1. Given that $U R = U/(L F) times 100$. With decreasing unemployed workers and increasing unemployment rate, then the labour force must fall proportionally faster than the number of unemployed people.

2. $"Unemployment rate" = "Unemployed" ÷ "Labour force" times "100" = 1474.9 ÷ 22579.7 times 100 = 6.53% → 6.5%.$ This matches the published 6.5%.

  $"Employment rate" = "Employed" ÷ "Population" 15+ times 100 = 21104.8 ÷ 34854.9 times 100 = 60.55% approx 60.6%.$ This matches the published 60.6%.

  $"Participation rate "= "Labour force" ÷ "Population" 15+ times 100 = 22579.7 ÷ 34854.9 times 100 = 64.78% approx 64.8%$ This matches the published 64.8%.

3. (i) Employed: $21104.8 - 21,173.1 = -68.3$ thousand, a change of -68300 people.

  (ii) Unemployed: $1474.9 - 1459.5 = +15.4$ thousand, a change of +15400 people.

  (iii) Verification:

  Change in labour force = $22579.7 - 22632.6 = - 52.9$ thousand, or -52900 people.
  Change in employment + change in unemployment = $- 68300 + 15400 = -52900$ people.
